# Poly(sodium-p-styrenesulfonate)-grafted UiO-66 composite membranes boosting highly efficient molecular separation for environmental remediation

Xi Quan Cheng1,2 · Songwei Li3,4 · Hongfei Bao5 · Xiaobin Yang3 · Zhixing Li1 $\cdot$ Yingjie Zhang1 $\cdot$ Kai Wang1 $\cdot$ Jun Ma7 · Asmat Ullah6 · Lu Shao3
Abstract
The lack of highly permeable and selective separation membranes hampers the application of the energy-efficient membrane separation process for water remediation and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in molecular scale. Nanoparticles have been widely applied for tailoring pore structures and surface properties of the molecular separation membranes, thereby enhancing separation performance. However, limited by non-ideal nanoparticle aggregation, it remains a big challenge to fabricate such membranes on a large scale. Herein, we designed a kind of highly dispersed nanoparticles through grafting poly(sodium-p-styrenesulfonate) (PSS) on metal organic frameworks UiO-66 and deployed them for tailoring structure of polyelectrolyte membrane (PEM) for water remediation and Pebax (PEBA) symmetrical membranes for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, respectively. Because of electrostatic interaction, the PSS-grafted-UiO-66 (UiO-66-PSS) demonstrated nearly $100\%$ dispersion in water and had an average particle size around $200\mathrm{nm}$ . Most interestingly, the introduction of the UiO-66-PSS finely tailored the affinity between membranes and target molecules, and the molecular structure of both PEM and PEBA membranes, thereby tripling the permeances of the membranes and slightly improving the selectivity of the membranes meanwhile. The PEM/UiO-66-PSS membranes demonstrated pure water permeance as high as $27.3\mathrm{L}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ with $\mathrm{Mg}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ rejection above $98.7\%$ . For $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, the PEBA/UiO-66-PSS membranes exhibited 414.5 barrer with selectivity about 53.8. Taken together, the highly dispersed UiO-66-PSS shows strong promise in constructing molecular separation membranes with high separation performance for environmental remediation.
Keywords  Highly dispersed nanoparticles $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Finely tailored membrane structures $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Highly permeable membranes $\cdot\cdot$ Water remediation $\cdot\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture
## 1  Introduction
Contaminants in molecular scale, such as dyes, antibiotic molecules and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , are hard to be cured and endanger global environment and development of human society [1–7]. Among all technologies, advanced membrane technologies are generally believed as one of the most efficient technologies to separate and collect the molecular scale contaminations because of their high separation efficiency, low energy consumption, high degree of automation and small footprint [4, 8–13]. However, it remains a big challenge to scale up the advanced membrane technologies for removal of molecular scale contaminates since the ubiquitous “trade-off” between the permeance and the selectivity of molecular separation membrane, especially for conventional polymeric membranes [1, 8–11]. Developing ideal molecular separation membranes with both high permeances and selectivity is crucial to realizing the practical application of advanced membrane technologies for molecular contaminant removal [1, 8, 11, 13].
Incorporating nanofillers/nanoparticles into polymeric separation membranes has been proven to be effective in developing molecular separation membranes with excellent separation performance, attracting significant attention in recent years [11, 14–17]. On the one hand, nanofillers can tailor the pore structure of the polymeric selective layer, thereby improving permeances significantly [18–24]. On the other hand, nanofillers may also adjust the interactions between polymeric selective layers and target fluids and confine the motion of the polymer chain within the selective layer, thereby enhancing the membrane selectivity and encumbering swelling or physical aging of as-fabricated membranes [15, 25–31]. To achieve excellent separation efficiency, the compatibility between the nanofillers and the polymeric membranes should be improved [32–36], since the addition of conventional inorganic nanoparticles might create ineffective voids (defects) which might lead to the decline in selectivity. Compared with conventional organic nanofillers, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have gained lots of attention because the MOFs, consisting of metal clusters and organic linkers, have the intrinsic rigidity of inorganic nanomaterials and functionality of organic materials [28, 32–37]. For instance, UiO-66, a water stable $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOFs, has been deployed for enhancing the separation performance for molecular separation through functionalizing the organic linkers [32–36, 38]. The incorporation of the modified UiO66 in the selective layer of the molecular separation membranes creates the additional passageways [32, 33] and improves affinity and the compatibility towards target molecules [34–36], thereby enhancing both the permeable properties and selectivity of the membranes. Although the incorporation of the modified UiO-66 has been proven to be effective in solving the trade-off phenomenon, the serious aggregation tendency of the nanofillers impedes scaling-up of the manufacture of such membranes [11, 16]. Unfortunately, there are only a few literatures focusing on the improvement of the dispersion of the nanofillers to achieve excellent molecular separation membranes, which is another important factor that affects the separation performance of the nanofillerenhanced polymeric membranes [39, 40].
Here, we design a kind of highly dispersed nanoparticles through grafting poly(sodium-p-styrene sulfonate) (PSS) on UiO-66 and apply this kind of highly dispersed nanoparticles in improving separation efficiency of the molecular separation membranes for water remediation and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, respectively. Because of electrostatic repulsion, the negatively charged and water-soluble PSS could significantly promote the dispersity of UiO-66 in aqueous solution (Fig. 1) and decrease the particle aggregation of UiO-66 obviously. The UiO-66-PSS nanoparticles are applied as nanofillers to enhance the separation performance of polyelectrolytes membrane (PEM) and Pebax (PEBA) symmetrical membranes towards water environment remediation and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, respectively. Unlike conventional nanoparticles, the UiO-66-PSS disperses uniformly in solvents and keeps stable over $24\mathrm{h}$ . Due to their intrinsic rigid structure, surface charge and hydrophilic functional groups, the UiO-66-PSS nanofillers will increase the filler interface interval within the polymeric selective layers, improve the surface charge and enhance the surface hydrophilicity of the PEM, thereby forming selective passageways that encourage water passing through the membranes while impede the penetration of inorganic salts and organic dyes when deployed for water environmental remediation. Moreover, when applied as nanofillers of PEBA membranes for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, the addition of UiO-66-PSS will also enlarge the interval between PEBA molecular chain and the UiO-66-PSS, and the sulfonic groups in the PSS chains could provide more $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective affinity sites, thereby promoting the permeability and the selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ synchronously.
Taken together, the highly dispersed UiO-66-PSS nanoparticles might show strong promise in tailoring performance of polymeric membranes for highly efficient molecular separation.
## 2  Experimental section
## 2.1  Chemicals
Triethylamine (TEA), methanol (MeOH), tetrahydrofuran (THF), Rose Bengal (RB), polyethylene glycol (PEG), dimethyl formamide (DMF), Congo Red (CR), Methylene blue (MeB), polyethylenimine (PEI), magnesium sulfate $\mathrm{(MgSO_{4})}$ ), magnesium chloride $\mathrm{(MgCl}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ ), sodium chloride $\mathrm{(NaCl)}$ , sodium sulfate $(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})$ , $\upalpha$ -bromoisobutyryl bromide (BiBB, $98\%$ ), sodium ascorbate $(>98\%)$ ), copper(II) bromide $\mathrm{CuBr}_{2}$ , $99\%$ ) and glutaraldehyde (GA) were analytical grade, purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd, which were used without further purification. Poly(sodium-p-styrenesulfonate) (PSS) and sodium styrene sulfonate (SS) were provided by Meryer (Shanghai) Chemical Technology Co., Ltd. Zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ , terephthalic acid, tris [2-(dimethylamino) ethyl] amine (Me6Tren) and 2-aminoterephthalic acid were analytical grade, which was supplied by Sigma-Aldrich (China mainland). Polyacrylonitrile (PAN) powders were purchased from Sinopec Qilu Petrochemical Company. HCl and $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ were ordered from Tianjin Kermel Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (China). The Pebax 1657 was purchased from Arkema (Paris, France).
## 2.2  Preparation and modification of UiO‑66
UiO-66 and UiO- $66\mathsf{-N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ were obtained as previously reported by Farha and his co-workers [41]. Briefly,
$50~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, 5 mmol $(1.25\ \mathrm{g})$ ) $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and $10~\mathrm{mL}$ concentrated HCl were added in a glass bottle and then sonicated for a certain time until fully dissolved, which was marked by solution A. Then, 5 mmol terephthalic acid or 2-aminoterephthalic acid was dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ DMF in another glass bottle (solution B). After that, solution B was added into solution A and the mixture was sonicated about $20\mathrm{min}$ for better mixing. The mixture was heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight to obtain UiO-66/UiO- $66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ solid powders. The resulting solid powders were filtered and washed first with DMF for one time. Then, the power was dispersed in MeOH and was separated by a centrifugal machine to remove the residual DMF, HCl and other unreacted reactants. Finally, the powders were heated to $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight under vacuum to remove the methanol completely and activate them.
UiO-66-PSS were synthesized through grafting PSS on the modified UiO-66 by atom transfer radical polymerization (ATRP). Firstly, the Br-modified $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 (UiO66-Br) was synthesized as reported by Xie et al. [39]. The UiO-66-PSS samples were obtained through an ATRP procedure using UiO-66-Br as the initiator. Typically, $\boldsymbol{0.3\mathrm{~g~}}$ of initiator and a certain content of monomers (SS) were sonicated with deionized water ( $\mathrm{100mL)}$ to form a uniform solution (marked as solution C). A total of $224\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{Me}_{6}\mathrm{Tre}$ n, $43.4\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ and $114\mathrm{mg}$ sodium ascorbate were dissolved in deionized water $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ) (marked as solution D). A light green suspension was formed after the mixture solution C and solution D, and continuous stirring for 24 at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After that, a green, transparent solution was obtained by separating the aforementioned suspension by a centrifugal machine at $\mathrm{RCF=0.4~g}$ for $2\mathrm{min}$ . Then, a light yellow solution was formed via purification of the green solution by a dialysis process (cut-off $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{W}=3500$ ). Finally, the light yellow solution was concentrated and freeze-dried to achieve yellow powders (UiO-66-PSS). The UiO-66-PSS mentioned in this study refers to that the weight feeding ratio of the monomer vs the initiator is 1:1.
## 2.3  Fabrication of molecular separation membranes
Preparation of molecular separation membranes for water remediation  PAN ultrafiltration substrates were developed through phase-inversion technique as reported everywhere [20, 42]. Briefly, the casting solution containing $18\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ PAN, $2\mathrm{wt}\%$ PEG and $80\mathrm{wt.}\%$ NMP was stirred and mixed in a three-necked flask under $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . After degassed for $12\mathrm{h}$ , the solution was poured onto a flat glass plate and then was cast by a $200\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ gaped steel blade after the casting solution was cooled down. Then, PAN ultrafiltration substrates were formed after immersing the glass plate in water coagulation for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Then, PAN ultrafiltration substrates were hydrophilized through successively immersing in $10\mathrm{wt.}\%\mathrm{NaOH}$ solution and 0.2 M HCl solution to obtain PAN-H substrates. Finally, the hydrolysed PAN substrates were rinsed with deionized water for three times and then soaked in deionized water for at least $12\mathrm{h}$ before use.
To prepare PEM incorporated with MOFs, the hydrolysed PAN ultrafiltration substrate (PAN-H) was firstly immersed in a $3~\mathrm{g/L}$ PEI solution for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . After being rinsed by deionized water for three times, the membrane was soaked with a $1\mathrm{g/L}$ anionic PSS (mixed with different types of MOFs) solution for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, the membrane was rinsed by deionized water again. The aforementioned steps were repeated for 10 cycles. Afterward, the membrane was soaked in the PEI solutions for $0.5\mathrm{h}$ to achieve positive charged surface. Finally, the obtained TFN membranes were preserved in deionized water after being crosslinked by $5\mathrm{\wt.\%}$ GA solution at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The PEI/ PSS self-assembly membranes, the UiO-66 incorporated self-assembly membranes and the UiO-66-PSS incorporated self-assembly membranes were named as “PEMs”, “PEM/UiO-66” and “PEM/UiO-66-PSS”, respectively, if no otherwise mentioned.
Preparation of molecular separation membranes for gas separation  A certain amount of UiO-66 or UiO-66-PSS was sonicated in the ethanol and water mixed solvents (mass ratio $=7{:}3$ ) for $0.5\mathrm{h}$ . Afterward, a certain amount of PEBA was dissolved into the aforementioned solution to form a uniform polymer matrix solution containing $5\mathrm{wt}\%$ MOFs, followed by heated with stirring and refluxing under $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . By casting the PEBA/MOF solution on a glass plate, the mixed matrix membranes were prepared after evaporation of the solvents at room temperature. Then, the mixed matrix membranes were dried in a vacuum oven at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . For comparison, the pristine PEBA membrane was fabricated following the similar procedure. The gas separation membranes were named as PEBA, PEBA/UiO-66 and PEBA/UiO-66-PSS, respectively.
## 2.4  Separation performance
Salts and dye separation performance  The permeances or fluxes of the TFN membranes were performed with a selfmade cross-flow cell. During filtration, the flow rate of the feed solution kept at $60{\mathrm{~L~h~}}^{-1}$ to minimize concentration polarization if no otherwise mentioned. Permeate samples were collected for a certain time and analysed, and the operation pressure of the system was kept at 5.0 bar. The water/ solution flux and water/solution permeance were calculated with the following equations:
$$
F=\frac{V}{A\times t}
$$
$$
{\mathrm{Permeance}}={\frac{F}{\Delta P}}
$$
where $F$ represents flux $(\mathrm{L~m}^{-2}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}),$ , $V\left(L\right)$ is defined as the volume of the solvent (or solution) passing through the membrane, $A$ represents the effective membrane area $(\mathbf{m}^{2})$ , $t$ is the testing time (h), and $\Delta P$ represents trans-membrane pressure (bar).
The solute rejections of TFN membranes were obtained using Eq. 3:
$$
R=(1-\frac{C_{\mathrm{p}}}{C_{\mathrm{f}}})
$$
where $C_{\mathfrak{p}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{f}}$ represent the solute concentrations in the permeate and the feed solution, respectively. For the separation of salts, the feed concentration was $1000\ \mathrm{mg\L^{-1}}$ , and the concentration was determined by an electrical conductivity meter (DHAUS, ST3100C-F). Dye concentration in water was determined by a UV–VIS CINTRA20-GBC apparatus under the maximum absorption wave number $\langle\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ of Congo $\operatorname{Red}=498\mathrm{nm}$ ). Each data point is an average of three repetitions, with $\pm5\%$ standard deviation.
Gas separation performance  Pure gas permeation measurements were conducted by a self-made constant-volume apparatus through the time-lag method. The pure gas permeability ( $P$ , Barrer) can be calculated by the following Eq. 4:
$$
\boldsymbol{P}=\boldsymbol{D}\times\boldsymbol{S}
$$
where $D$ represents the diffusion coefficient (diffusivity, $\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{s})$ , $s$ is defined as the sorption coefficient (solubility, $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\:(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}\:\mathrm{cm}\:\mathrm{Hg})$ . The ideal selectivity of gas A over gas B $(\alpha_{\mathrm{a/b}})$ represents the ratio of their permeabilities as follows:
$$
\alpha_{\frac{\mathrm{A}}{\mathrm{B}}}={\frac{P_{\mathrm{A}}}{P_{\mathrm{B}}}}=\left[{\frac{D_{\mathrm{A}}}{D_{\mathrm{B}}}}\right]\times\left[{\frac{S_{\mathrm{A}}}{S_{\mathrm{B}}}}\right]
$$
The gases were measured in the flowing order, $\mathrm{H}_{2},\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under 3.5 bar at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The gas permeability could be calculated by the following Eq. 6:
$$
P={\frac{273\times10^{10}}{760}}{\frac{V l}{A T{\left(p_{2}\times{\frac{76}{14.7}}\right)}}}{\frac{d_{\mathrm{p}}}{d_{\mathrm{t}}}}
$$
where $d_{\mathrm{p}}/d_{\mathrm{t}}$ is the steady increase rate of downstream-pressure, l represents the membrane thickness (cm), $V$ is defined as the volume of the downstream chamber $(\mathrm{cm}^{3})$ . $T$ is defined as the operating temperature (K), $A$ represents the effective membrane area $(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ , and $p_{2}$ represents the upstream operating pressure (psi).
Using the diffusion time lag $\left(\theta\right)$ extrapolated from the plot of pressure with time at steady state to the time axis, the diffusivity can be calculated by Eq. 7:
$$
D=\frac{l^{2}}{6\theta}
$$
The solubility can be simply calculated by Eq. 4 as mentioned above.
The characterization and other properties of the membranes are shown in supporting information.
## 3  Results and discussion
## 3.1  Characterization and properties of the PSS‑grafted MOFs
PSS was grafted on the UiO-66 through atomic transfer free polymerization (ATRP) technique to improve the dispersion of UiO-66 and tailor the interaction between the modified UiO-66 and the polyelectrolyte selective layer of the PEMs. The PSS chains are successfully grafted on the UiO-66 since the modified UiO-66 exhibits different chemical and physical properties after the grafting process. Interestingly, the nanoparticles show excellent dispersion in water and the dispersion of the nanoparticles is even stable after $24\mathrm{h}$ (Fig. S1). The peaks around $900{\sim}1190\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ own to the stretching vibration of sulfonic groups (Fig. S2) [43, 44]. The O1s peak around $531.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ belongs to ${\bf C}{\bf-O}$ bond and $\scriptstyle{\mathbf{C}}={\mathbf{O}}$ bond, which is assigned to –COOH group (Fig. 2a) [45]. After grafting, a new peak around $533.3\mathrm{eV}$ appears, which is attributed to the sulfonic group in the PSS chains (Fig. 2b) [39]. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of UiO-66 and their derivates show that the sharp peaks from the UiO-66 become broad and diffuse after grafting PSS chains on the UiO-66 because of the amorphous PSS diffraction (Fig. 2c) [46]. The decomposition temperature of UiO-66-PSS decreased around $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , indicating that the successful grafting of the PSS chains on the UiO-66 (Fig. 2d). When the temperature reached $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the mass of UiO-66- PSS decreased by $25\%$ , which might be due to the degradation of PSS chains. Moreover, the UiO-66-PSS shows a negative surface charge due to the existence of $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{Na}$ group (Fig. 2e), which also substantiates the successful grafting of PSS.
The UiO-66-PSS shows smaller mean effective nanoparticle size $(200~\mathrm{nm})$ and narrower nanoparticle size distribution compared with UiO-66 $(650~\mathrm{nm})$ (Fig. 2f). Moreover, TEM images show that the UiO-66 particles aggregate seriously and consist of many round solids. By contrast, a thin layer $(\sim20~\mathrm{nm})$ clearly arose on the UiO-66-PSS (Fig. 2g\~i). The grafting of PSS chains on the UiO-66 surface increases electrostatic repulsion that decreases the aggregation tendency of the nanoparticles, thereby promoting the dispersion of the nanoparticles [39, 47]. With excellent dispersion, small nanoparticle size and narrow size distribution, which are crucial for developing advanced molecular separation membranes, the as-prepared UiO-66-PSS theoretically show strong promise in tailoring molecular separation performance for both water remediation and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture [15, 18–20, 48, 49].
## 3.2  Deployed as additives in molecular separation membranes for water remediation
With pore size in the range of $0.5{\sim}2.0~\mathrm{nm}$ , nanofiltration membranes can separate small active organic molecules and multi-valent ions from water [10, 11, 50, 51]. Here, we show that the addition of PSS-grafted UiO-66 particles can finely tailor the membrane structures and surface properties, thereby facilitating water passing through the polyelectrolyte (PE) membranes (PEM) fabricated by self-assembly without compromising rejection efficiency towards ions and dyes. The surface morphology of PE selective layer is quite smooth (Fig. 3 a and d), while new mountain-andvalley structures arise on the selective layers after addition of UiO-66 and UiO-66-PSS (Fig. $3\mathrm{b}$ and c). In detail, the UiO-66-PSS results in a uniform distribution in the PE selective layer, whereas partly aggregation of UiO-66 is observed (Fig. 3 b and c). Similarly, a small number of large bulges are observed from the topology of the PEM/UiO66 membranes via atomic force microscope (AFM) images while plenty of small bulges are found on the PEM/UiO-66- PSS surface, which also confirms the partly aggregation of UiO-66 and the uniform distribution of UiO-66-PSS. The excellent dispersity, small particle size, and narrow nanoparticle size distribution of UiO-66-PSS contribute to their uniform distribution and benign surface roughness. Interestingly, the incorporation of the nanofillers also decreases the water contact angle which might be ascribed to the increment of surface roughness after incorporation of nanofillers and the benign hydrophilicity of the nanofillers [52, 53]. Moreover, the positive surface charge of the membranes (Fig. S5) increases significantly $(30\%)$ after incorporation of the UiO-66-PSS because the negative UiO-66-PSS can adsorb more PEI molecules during the self-assembly process. The better wettability and higher surface charge might endue the membranes with better separation performance and anti-fouling capability towards positive solutes [52–54]. In addition, pore structure of the membranes is also finely adjusted by the incorporation of UiO-66-PSS nanoparticles, which is another crucial factor that affects the permeance and rejection performance of the molecular separation membranes (Fig. 4 a and b) [54–56]. The mean effective pore radius of hydrolysed polyacrylonitrile (PAN)
substrate is about $2.98\ \mathrm{nm}$ . After formation of PE selective layer, the pore radius of the obtained membranes decreased to $0.30~\mathrm{nm}$ . The pore of the membranes became smaller and narrower further after incorporating UiO-66 or UiO66-PSS nanoparticles. The increase in surface charge and the decrease in pore size, which is caused by incorporation of nanofillers, leads to the increasement of the rejections towards dyes and salts as expected. Interestingly, these changes also induce significant increment of water permeances. Especially, the water permeance of the NF membranes incorporated with UiO-66-PSS increased by $280\%$ $(27.3\mathrm{L}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{bar}^{-1})$ ; meanwhile, the rejections towards $\mathrm{{\bfMg}S O_{4}}$ and CR also increased from $92.3\%$ and $96.7\%$ to $98.7\%$ and $99.9\%$ , respectively (Fig. 4c, Table 1). The incorporation of nanofillers might create plenty of interval pores accessible for more water transportation [48, 49, 57, 58]. The distribution of UiO-66-PSS in the selective layer of the PEM/UiO-66-PSS membranes is much better than that of UiO-66, which creates more interfacial pores, thereby boosting water permeances. Besides, the better wettability of the PEM/UiO-66-PSS membranes also contributes to their higher permeability. The order of rejections of the obtained membranes is $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}{>}\mathbf{MgSO}_{4}{>}\mathbf{Na}_{2}\mathbf{SO}_{4}{>}\mathbf{NaCl},$ As the pore size of the PEM and PEM/UiO-66-PSS are around $0.30~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is similar with the radius of the hydrated radius of ${\mathrm{SO}_{4}}^{2-}$ and $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , the rejection of different salts is dependent on the combination of Donnan effects and the steric effects [38, 48]. Excitingly, when deployed for separation of CR, the UiO-66-PSS incorporated PEMs exhibit high rejection above $99.9\%$ , good antifouling performance with water recovery above $98\%$ (Fig.  4d) and excellent stable flux as $110.4~\mathrm{L~m}^{-2}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ under 5.0  bar operation pressure during $240\mathrm{~h~}$ of continuous operation (Fig. S6d). Notably, both the permeances and the rejections of the PEM/UiO-66-PSS membranes are higher than that of the aforementioned modified MOFs incorporated thinfilm composite membranes used for separating salts and dyes from water, which might be attributed to the excellent dispersion of the UiO-66-PSS. Besides, the performance of the PEM/UiO-66-PSS TFN membranes also outperforms most of state-of-the-art molecular separation membranes reported previously (Fig. S7) S12−S24.
## 3.3  Deployed as additives in gas separation membranes for $\pmb{\mathrm{co}}_{2}$ capture
In the field of clean energy, MOFs are especially popular in hydrogen storage, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation, and hydrocarbon separation. MOFs and polymer matrix have been widely utilized to prepare mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) which combine the advantages of organic matrix and porous fillers to improve the gas separation efficiency of polymer-based separation membranes and is a promising alternative to intrinsic polymeric membranes [59, 60]. In addition, the functionalization of organic ligands in MOFs can introduce specific polar groups to further enhance the interactions with the target gas and the interface compatibility with polymer matrix [61]. Since the MOFs we prepared have the proper pore size $(\sim0.5\:\mathrm{nm})$ and undergo chemical post-modification, they are expected to be used in the field of gas separation to further improve the gas transport capacity of the MMMs. We dispersed a certain amount of UiO-66 or UiO-66-PSS nanocrystals in PEBA solution to prepare MMMs to elevate its gas separation performance. Adopting the time-lag technique, a pure gas permeation experiment was performed at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under 3.5 bar. As shown in Fig. 5a, the permeability of as-prepared PEBA-based MMMs toward the tested gas is enhanced significantly. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability is increased by $151.1\%$ to 277.1 barrer (PEBA/UiO-66) and $276\%$ to 414.5 barrer (PEBA/UiO-66-PSS), respectively. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability of PEBA/UiO-66-PSS can surpass the 2008 upper bound easily and is superior to many UiO-66 doped mixed matrix membranes (Fig. S8). Moreover, the gas diffusivity and solubility of the MMMs are further analysed which is shown in Fig. 5 c and d. The increased permeability of MMMs, which is fabricated with functionalized-UiO-66, is mainly attributed to the enhanced gas diffusivity. The formation of a disordered arrangement of polymer molecular chains, which owes to the incorporation of the MOFs (UiO-66 or UiO-66-PSS), increases the interval between the molecular chains and thus promotes the direct transport of gas by Knudsen diffusion [62]. Obviously, the promotion in membrane permeability from PEBA/UiO-66-PSS greatly surpasses that from PEBA/UiO-66 with the same filler loading ratio due to varying the functional moieties of UiO-66 in the MMMs.
Most importantly, in this work, both the permeability and selectivities of the membranes increase significantly after the incorporation of UiO-66-PSS (Fig. 5b). In order to further explore the underlying reason, the solubility and diffusivity are evaluated (Fig. 5 c and d). Since the porous MOF material has a strong adsorption effect toward sizematched gas, polar groups such as $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ within UiO-66- PSS exhibit selective affinity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . This is due to the fact that noncovalent bond exists between the membranes and the target molecules. The electrostatic interactions and hydrogen bonding interactions existing between $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ increase the affinity between the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and the membranes, thereby promoting the diffusivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ greatly [61, 63]. In addition, the flexible hydrophilic PEBA chains can avoid defect formation of non-selective voids in the MMMs. Thus, the solubility of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ increases slightly with the incorporation of MOF, while diffusivity enhances obviously. Furthermore, MOFs (UiO-66 or UiO-66-PSS) that are evenly dispersed and well combined with PEBA can form efficient gas transport channels in the membrane, thus greatly promoting the diffusion of gas in the membrane, that is, the diffusion capacities of both $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ are improved. The good interaction between the additional MOFs (UiO-66 and UiO-66-PSS) and the PEBA leads to the further improvement of the affinity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and the decrease of the affinity for $\Nu_{2}$ . Therefore, with the addition of UiO-66 or UiO-66-PSS, the solubility of ${\bf N}_{2}$ decreased, while the diffusivity increased. In summary, the incorporation of the UiO-66-PSS increases the permeability of the PEBA membranes and the affinity towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , thereby greatly and synchronously increasing both the permeability and selectivity of MMMs.
of properties (permeance, rejection, flux) of the membranes at least, and each value of properties of the membranes was measured twice at least to ensure the deviation of each value is less than $5\%$ . Operation conditions: pressure 3.5 bar, temperature $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
## 4  Conclusion
In this study, a highly water dispersed PSS grafted UiO-66 were successfully synthesized through ATRP-induced grafting. After grafting, the nanoparticles exhibited a negative surface charge by virtue of tailored $-S O_{3}\mathrm{Na}$ groups and their particle size decreased to $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . In addition, the UiO66-PSS demonstrates nearly $100\%$ dispersion in water and distributes uniformly in the polymeric membranes creating additional molecular passageways and boosting the selective affinity between the membranes and the target separation molecules. As a result, the permeable property of the membranes increases by $200\%$ , and the selectivity (rejection) of the membranes increases slightly as well. Taken together, the highly dispersed nanofiller shows strong promise in constructing advanced membranes with high comprehensive separation performance for environmental remediation.
